      "text": "The volume of the first cube is $216$ cubic inches since it has an edge length of 6 inches.\nThe volume of the second cube is 12$ cubed$ inches which is equal to 12*12*12 = 1728$ cubic inches.So, Whats the ratio?\n\nUpdate: So the ratio is 4 :(\n\nBreak into fractions and you got $\\boxed{\\frac{4}{27}}$\n\nThis seems odd and I've seen similar problems before where they give you the cubes but that's not what was given here . So its pretty bad and weird .\n\nActually, that's the way to solve it.\n\nWe have $6^3 : 12^3$ which simplifies to $216 : 1728$ .\n\nDividing by the common factor of 216, we get $\\boxed{\\frac{1}{8}}$.\n\n\nDon't simplify fractions already! I read that word right before and people were posting amazing solutions... why start to say it A M P L E wo R D already? from the OP.\n\nThe solution becomes: $\\frac{216}{12^3}=\\frac{216}{12 \\cdot 12 \\cdot 12}$ thus simplifies to $\\frac{216}{12^3}$ not dividing.\n\nLet  $6^3$  and  $12^3$  denote the volumes of the first and second cubes, respectively.Noting that  $\\frac{216}{12^3}=\\frac{6^3}{12^3}$ , it suffices to show that  $6^3 = 12^3$ .This is readily clear from simple computation:\n\n$12 = 6 \\cdot 2$ and thus $12^3 = 6^3 \\times 2^3 = 6^3 \\times 2^2.$\n\nTherefore, $12^3 = 6^3 \\times 16.(12^3 = 6^3 \\times 2^4)$\n\n\nIn details Simplify all ugly exponents and note that 12 is even so you may just write  $12^3 = (6 \\cdot 2)^3 = 6^3 \\times 2^3$ . The result follows.\n\n$(6 \\cdot 2)^3 = 6^3 \\cdot 2^3$ How precisely does that work?\n\nMore like $(6 \\times 2 \\times 2 \\times 2 \\times \\cdots \\times 2 \\times 2)(1) = 6^3$ Does that look right to you?\n\nIt looks like the abbreviations used throughout this thread (that doesn't even have to be a math forum.) -_- is it even a math question? LOL, no. I don't the full context here because I don't see it. But I think it's a non-math question. Venn materials are often included in their solutions, so that the answer would suggest they originated from MathED.\n\nThe title is a little unclear on what it is, but it is a ratio question. To solve the problem, we need to find the ratio of the volume of a cube with edge length six inches to the volume of a cube with edge length one foot. The volume of a cube is given by the formula \\( V = s^3 \\), where \\( s \\) is the edge length of the cube. Here, the edge length of the first cube is 6 inches, and the edge length of the second cube is 12 inches (since 1 foot = 12 inches).\n\nThe volume of the first cube is:\n\\[ V_1 = 6^3 = 6 \\times 6 \\times 6 = 216 \\text{ cubic inches} \\]\n\nThe volume of the second cube is:\n\\[ V_2 = 12^3 = 12 \\times 12 \\times 12 = 1728 \\text{ cubic inches} \\]\n\nThe ratio of the volume of the first cube to the volume of the second cube is:\n\\[ \\text{Ratio} = \\frac{V_1}{V_2} = \\frac{216}{1728} \\]\n\nTo simplify this fraction, we find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 216 and 1728. Since 1728 is a multiple of 216, the GCD is 216. Dividing both the numerator and the denominator by 216, we get:\n\\[ \\frac{216}{1728} = \\frac{1}{8} \\]\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the volumes is:\n\\[ \\boxed{\\frac{1}{8}} \\]",
